"""Original medicines and substance-use drill; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
A medicine is a substance used for a medical purpose such as|prevention, diagnosis or treatment|guaranteeing the same effect in everyone|replacing all medical assessment|making every risk impossible|Medicines can have medical purposes; effects depend on appropriate use and individual circumstances.
A medicine prescribed for one person|is not automatically suitable for another|is always safe for everyone with similar symptoms|should be shared whenever requested|cannot interact with other medicines|Prescriptions consider individual circumstances, so sharing can be unsafe.
Which factor can affect a medicine's effects?|dose and individual circumstances|the colour of the package alone|the name of the pharmacy alone|the day of a sports match|Dose, route, other medicines and individual characteristics can affect effects.
A person uncertain about their prescription should|ask a qualified prescriber or pharmacist|guess a new dose|copy a friend's prescription|follow an unrelated advertisement|A qualified professional can clarify how to use a medicine appropriately.
The medicine label can provide|instructions and safety information|a complete diagnosis of every illness|a guarantee of no side effects|a prescription for another person|Labels provide information, but do not replace individual advice.
A concerning reaction to a medicine should be|discussed promptly with a qualified professional; severe reactions may need urgent help|ignored in every case|treated by automatically doubling the dose|diagnosed from the package colour|Concerning reactions need appropriate assessment, and severe reactions may be urgent.
Changing prescribed treatment solely because of an advertisement is|unsafe without appropriate professional advice|always necessary|a complete assessment|a guarantee of improvement|Advertisements cannot assess a person's condition or interactions.
Why should prescription medicines not be shared?|People may have different conditions, doses and interactions|Similar symptoms always have the same cause|Every medicine is safe for all people|One label diagnoses all illnesses|Different people may need different treatments and can have different risks.
Antibiotics act against|susceptible bacteria|all viruses|only nutritional deficiencies|every cause of fever|Antibiotics target susceptible bacteria, not viruses.
Antibiotics do not treat|viral infections such as the common cold|bacterial infections when organisms are susceptible|some bacterial infections|infections caused by susceptible bacteria|Antibiotics do not act against viruses that cause the common cold.
Unnecessary antibiotic use can contribute to|antimicrobial resistance|guaranteed immunity against all infections|the elimination of all bacteria|a complete diagnosis|Misuse and overuse can contribute to the emergence and spread of resistance.
Antibiotic resistance refers to|microorganisms no longer responding to medicines that once worked|a person's decision not to exercise|a joint resisting movement|all viruses becoming bacteria|Resistance arises when microorganisms change and medicines become less effective.
Leftover antibiotics should not be shared because|the other person may need different assessment and treatment|all symptoms have one cause|leftovers are a universal prescription|medical assessment is unnecessary|Symptoms and suitable treatments differ, and sharing can be harmful.
A clinician prescribing an antibiotic considers|the condition, likely cause and individual circumstances|only the package colour|only the person's preferred team|only an unrelated patient's diagnosis|Appropriate treatment depends on clinical context and the person.
Alcohol can impair|judgement, coordination and reaction time|only the names of muscles|only bone classification|only a match schedule|Alcohol can impair abilities needed for safe decisions and movement.
Driving after drinking alcohol is unsafe because alcohol can|slow reactions and impair judgement|guarantee better concentration|remove all road hazards|make traffic rules unnecessary|Alcohol can impair driving-related abilities and increase injury risk.
The effect of alcohol can vary with|amount, rate of consumption and individual factors|only the label's colour|only the date on a calendar|no factors at all|Effects depend on the dose, timing and individual circumstances.
Combining alcohol with medicines|can create interactions and requires appropriate advice|is automatically safe in every case|eliminates side effects|turns every medicine into an antibiotic|Alcohol can interact with medicines; consult an appropriately qualified professional.
A person who is difficult to wake after drinking and has abnormal breathing|needs urgent help|should be left alone to sleep without checking|should be forced to drink coffee|can be diagnosed from a textbook|Unresponsiveness or abnormal breathing requires urgent emergency assistance.
Tobacco smoke exposure increases the risk of|cardiovascular and respiratory disease and cancer|a guaranteed absence of illness|only sports injuries|every person having an identical outcome|Tobacco smoke increases risk of several diseases, though individual outcomes vary.
Second-hand smoke|can harm people exposed to another person's smoke|affects only the smoker|is guaranteed harmless outdoors and indoors|is a treatment for lung disease|People exposed to second-hand tobacco smoke can be harmed.
Nicotine can cause|dependence|complete immunity to illness|all bacterial infections to disappear|a guaranteed absence of withdrawal|Nicotine is addictive and can lead to dependence.
A nicotine or vaping product should not automatically be assumed safe because|risks depend on the product and user, and such products are not harmless|every familiar product is harmless|labels always provide a complete medical assessment|nicotine cannot cause dependence|Familiarity or a label does not eliminate risks.
Support for stopping tobacco use can include|counselling and appropriate professional advice|stigma as the only response|withholding all information|assuming every person needs the same plan|Counselling and individual advice can support cessation.
A product labelled herbal|is not automatically safe for every person or in every combination|is guaranteed harmless|cannot interact with medicines|is a complete diagnosis|A natural or herbal label does not guarantee safety or absence of interactions.
An unknown substance may|contain an unexpected ingredient or strength|always match an informal seller's claim|have an identical effect on everyone|be safe because it is familiar to a friend|Unknown products may differ from what the user expects.
Mixing substances can|increase risks and interactions|guarantee that harmful effects cancel out|eliminate the need for medical help|make an unknown dose predictable|Interactions between substances can increase harm.
Dependence can involve|difficulty controlling use and a strong drive to use|a guaranteed identical experience for everyone|only a person's clothing|a sports classification|Dependence can involve difficulty controlling use, though individual experiences differ.
Tolerance or withdrawal|may occur with some forms of dependence|must occur identically in every person|proves all substances have the same effects|is a diagnosis based on appearance alone|Tolerance and withdrawal may occur, but experiences vary.
Some withdrawal syndromes|can be dangerous and require qualified advice|are always harmless|should be managed identically without assessment|never involve physical symptoms|Some withdrawal syndromes can be dangerous; seek individual advice.
A diagnosis of a substance-use disorder|requires appropriate assessment|can always be made from a person's appearance|is established by any single stereotype|is the same as a competition score|An appropriate professional assessment considers the individual and relevant criteria.
Non-judgemental communication can|support a person in seeking help|guarantee immediate recovery|replace all clinical care|make every situation identical|Respectful support can reduce barriers to seeking care.
Stigma may|discourage people from seeking assistance|guarantee access to treatment|prove the cause of dependence|replace accurate information|Stigma can create barriers to care and support.
Treatment for substance-use problems|can vary by substance and individual|is identical for every person|never involves professional support|is established by a label alone|Care options depend on the substance and the person's circumstances.
A clear refusal and leaving an unsafe situation|can be part of a safety plan when feasible|guarantee that every situation is easy to leave|make trusted support unnecessary|replace emergency services|A refusal and departure can help, but safety planning must consider the circumstances.
A person with abnormal breathing after possible substance exposure|needs urgent emergency help|should be forced to eat|should be ignored until morning|can be diagnosed from a label alone|Abnormal breathing is an emergency; call local services and follow instructions.
When responding to a suspected substance emergency, a bystander should|consider their own safety and follow dispatcher instructions|enter a dangerous area regardless of risk|force food into an unresponsive person's mouth|delay calling for help to search for a cure|Protect yourself, call for help and follow dispatcher instructions.
An unresponsive person should not be|forced to eat, drink or take medication|monitored while help is arranged|reported to emergency services|assisted within the responder's training|Forcing anything into an unresponsive person's mouth can be dangerous.
Legal availability of a product|does not by itself prove it is harmless|guarantees no interactions|means its dose never matters|replaces safety advice|Legal status and safety are different questions.
A reliable substance-use lesson should|present evidence, acknowledge context and avoid stigma|claim every user has identical needs|promise all risks can be removed by one action|discourage appropriate professional help|Evidence, individual circumstances and non-stigmatising language support useful education.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
